lse. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186461070 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186461071 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186461072 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162639 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162639 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162639 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is positive-definite\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162639 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a18cc33-e8b9-51a0-9ec6-b247bdd1e062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162639 #edb/ref 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162855 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162855 #edb/ref 1061 30543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162855 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6be8e550-b338-5637-a624-ef1b87eabd9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162855 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162895 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162895 #edb/ref 1061 30875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162895 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca8c0245-0936-52e2-ab35-4e10d9592791" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162895 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162896 #edb/ref 1061 30876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d8db3b7-e619-5958-9482-0acdf61cd3e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162896 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162897 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162897 #edb/ref 1061 30877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162897 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20343d54-d701-5e59-9cab-48adc9d25870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162897 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162898 #edb/ref 1061 30878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5cc719a5-ab07-5159-9a43-3394f7193bc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162899 #edb/ref 1061 30879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d2fe4efb-5814-5b5d-a57c-b5cb76832908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163054 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163054 #edb/ref 1061 37271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163054 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b84589b-e820-5864-af80-1c00cdcc8be5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163054 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163055 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163055 #edb/ref 1061 37272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163055 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecf1e76d-67dd-5f72-a6ec-7fba2fc8aff6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163055 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163230 #edb/ref 1064 "In certain applications involving quadratic forms, an important question is whether a particular quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)$ is *always* positive or *always* negative.\n\nWe classify quadratic forms using the following terminology:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) > 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form\n\n$$\nS(\\mathbf{x})=8x_1^2+3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis positive-definite since\n$8x_1^2+3x_2^2 > 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/52/52e43ebb6fa34f40a73358f88aed62846332126c4fe080072c211945f54b412a.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) <     0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form \n\n$$\nT(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2-3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis negative-definite since\n$-x_1^2-3x_2^2 \\lt 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a86f80966c04f960823db91bfc8711eef2bf22a81502e69b5048afc5b4799cb2.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **indefinite** if \n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_1) > 0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ and\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_2) <     0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form\n\n$$\nR(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+2x_2^2-6x_1x_2\n$$\n\nis indefinite. Substituting $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {0}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0-0 \\\\  & =1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+2-6 \\\\  & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163230 #edb/ref 1065 4889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163230 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c6542a88-fba4-557b-ae08-48d95747b079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163230 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163241 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with a symmetric matrix $A$,  we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive,\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nUsing the same criteria, we can also classify the symmetric matrix $A$ as positive-definite, positive semi-definite, etc.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nComputing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +6) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4,\\,-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) =  \\mathbf{x}^T A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163241 #edb/ref 1065 4952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163241 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c4b47113-5633-5c1b-bb17-e6ec70b5a550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163241 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Eigenvalues to Classify Quadratic Forms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163242 #edb/ref 1064 "Let ![](images/c6/c6f4a56eed9073b3541e4398db9fc99f2e8ca94f651762e1f650bfe54865ca49.svg) for ![](images/16/1621ad9f5426c8fe19e2d5a00c5f92a60420aeaed7bfe623db70c9ab4e57527d.svg)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) \n\nAccording to **Sylvester's criterion**, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg) we have ![](images/03/039aff55ff839029e398761cdb94f09b8c2203413637d6f41ca9c680659cc8d2.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is odd, and ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is even.\n\nIn particular, if ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is negative-definite, then the leading principal minors alternate in sign, with the *first* leading principal minors being negative.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n\n![](images/bc/bc72eb8a57a1af3a4cc1526c071f7611131359d87b1ed56417e626aad662c9cb.svg)\n\nLet's compute its leading leading principal minors:\n\n![](images/84/844bf128565ccaeee211fcbeca2179b870901879303ce3e911956d65b468926f.svg)\n\nAll leading principal minors are positive. Therefore, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) is positive-definite." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163242 #edb/ref 1065 4953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163242 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "721cf6ec-0dbe-515f-ae9f-63c855607e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163242 #edb/ref 1067 "Sylvester's Criterion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163318 #edb/ref 1064 "There are two further classifications of quadratic forms that turn out to be useful:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form $A(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is positive semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not positive-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ \n\n![](images/8d/8d0adf62e90acc2f5a2a3fdbad2b87fc53e4f50145e38d8120cd932282afbb42.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ \n\nFor example, the form $B(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is negative semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not negative-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ \n\n![](images/76/76feacaad709f2f3a3c1dac8c0ee88aa461aed827270d0127f61de38d95489b6.png)\n\nIt's also worth noting that\n\n- any positive-definite quadratic form is positive semi-definite, and\n\n- any negative-definite quadratic form is negative semi-definite." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163318 #edb/ref 1065 6137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163318 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f10e579-6a9a-5929-95df-033a67f11e7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163318 #edb/ref 1067 "Positive and Negative Semi-Definite Quadratic Forms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163319 #edb/ref 1064 "The **leading principal minors** of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix are the ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) determinants formed by the first ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) rows and first ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) columns of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) for ![](images/02/025dfd41d08c5cd679c6635316e6d92c7b612c1db8a0d5c88ca3633eb5dc3d0d.svg) The leading principal minors of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) are denoted ![](images/d4/d4d4f854b6186e5a619b024a48f4017756b1de3fcf8f3c90d80e5a251bc3de93.svg)\n\nNote that:\n\n- the first leading principal minors ![](images/e9/e983ef296ac0b20ed0beb7771d7616ab810886d775bd8e343f03f78c757ee678.svg) equals ![](images/1c/1caa82c7c23331cc78782a151b62bafd5f6d9acee12c7ae29dc74556ebc16c1c.svg) and\n\n- the ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg)th leading principal minors ![](images/a2/a27bb42bcd37b2e4ba1416cff413d166b960a686a26ca5085da9be8661d8faf8.svg) equals ![](images/3a/3a950a6005df11dc3df83b40a796c8e5ce962b591e040207bb961da532ec92dd.svg)\n\nFor example, consider the matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by\n\n![](images/26/261bd51313b7630f2c14550d0318e1094a7915316c165e8555ddd77148b8b26d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is a ![](images/59/599d5897394ff1110f6a3bac7053c6836d231de17effad76a1c633d1aed81b33.svg) matrix, it has ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) leading principal minors. Let's compute them:\n\n- The first leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first row and first column of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n![](images/9a/9ab81179136bdf16c09d64def927c7175884692ecd6f359143bb7333e91b3a2b.svg)\n\n- The second leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) rows and first ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) columns of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n![](images/03/032d2edce1d92da3a0b49aa2e582f61896280571c9fd2c1cc277b7cc77af353e.svg)\n\n- The third leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) rows and first ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) columns of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n\n![](images/9d/9d1f53a40f9e6dfcb44f231287b67b70a42d1cd10dbf99c803517f21af7265eb.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163319 #edb/ref 1065 6138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163319 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8136146f-4191-5b4d-be2d-f33bf2b8d1f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163319 #edb/ref 1067 "The Leading Principal Minors of a Square Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450720 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186269225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450721 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186269225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450722 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186269225 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269225 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269225 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186269225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "906865b3-32d2-5d7f-b7fc-d38ff3d60bed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269225 #edb/ref 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and  III are false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, neither the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ nor the matrix $A$ is positive-definite or negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470473 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470474 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470475 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360479 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360479 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360479 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n- **I.** $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- **II.** $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- **III.** $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360479 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96ce050e-6292-5b2a-b7c7-0c1f7f51192d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360479 #edb/ref 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-1)^{2}-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1-9 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & =(-1)^{2}+3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+27 \\\\  & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452877 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452878 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452879 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360482 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R(x)=8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite\n- **II.** $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08e6755d-cc77-5492-aef8-539543b12cc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360482 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-(2{x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2})<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452889 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452890 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452891 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360499 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360499 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is indefinite" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13d1d5cf-031d-5ebf-8e4c-d550f344afdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360499 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452892 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452893 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452894 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360500 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360500 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=2$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81d008a7-fd35-5fff-9f9d-fc0e8e60bfec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360500 #edb/ref 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452904 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452905 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452906 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360504 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-6$ and $det(A)<0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0bcde67-e6f1-5859-87e0-ff664264d2ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360504 #edb/ref 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186428883 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428883 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3127->990. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428883 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050001 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448786 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448786 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448786 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8385fb50-741c-52a9-b66d-7f14ebf47fdb" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448787 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448787 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c9d11be8-de44-538d-b685-8fbb9971c1c1" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448788 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448788 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 1